1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,056
Where does a card number live, when the payments product must never see one?

2
00:00:04,106 --> 00:00:10,968
Everything that touches a card number is in P C I D S S scope, the strictest audit in payments.

3
00:00:11,018 --> 00:00:17,976
So all of it lives in one unit, cards: the card vault, the card frame, and the Uzcard and Humo adapters.

4
00:00:18,026 --> 00:00:26,280
ecom, the payments product, holds only card tokens and masked numbers, so ecom and any future caller stay out of that scope.

5
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:33,792
The two units share nothing: each has its own database, its own Vault, its own Keycloak and its own monitoring.

6
00:00:33,842 --> 00:00:42,864
Cards never knows its callers: a caller is only a name in cards' inputs, plus a client certificate that cards' own certificate authority signed.

7
00:00:42,914 --> 00:00:49,536
Another caller, a mobile backend for example, changes cards' inputs, never its code or its deployment.

8
00:00:49,586 --> 00:00:58,464
ecom knows cards only by inputs too: the cards URLs, cards' certificate authority, its own client certificate, and one secret.

9
00:00:58,514 --> 00:01:07,008
Every call between services is mutual T L S, and it carries a token from cards' own Keycloak with the role the operation needs.

10
00:01:07,058 --> 00:01:10,920
Cards has two doors, and each lets through only what it must.

11
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:18,984
The payer door serves only the card frame: the payer's browser loads it and posts the card, and every other path answers four oh four.

12
00:01:19,034 --> 00:01:28,656
Think of a T L S connection as a sealed envelope: S N I is the address written on it, and a certificate is the key that opens it.

13
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:38,496
The partner door reads only the address, passes the envelope on unopened to the card vault, an adapter or Keycloak, and refuses any address it doesn't know.

14
00:01:38,546 --> 00:01:42,744
ecom gets its certificate the way a person gets a passport.

15
00:01:42,794 --> 00:01:49,248
ecom fills in the application, a C S R, with its own private key, which never leaves ecom.

16
00:01:49,298 --> 00:02:01,560
Cards, the passport office, stamps it with its seal and hands back three things: the certificate, cards' own certificate authority to recognise the real cards, and one secret for cards' Keycloak.

17
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:09,240
Each allowed caller has its own roles, its own origins whose pages may embed the frame, and its own session limits.

18
00:02:09,290 --> 00:02:17,568
Fraud limits count refused cards and codes per card, per BIN and per payer address, each within the configured limit and window.

19
00:02:17,618 --> 00:02:23,760
Card numbers stay in one unit, behind two narrow doors, and every caller is only an input.
